# The composed layers whose own totals cannot be differenced. One row per composed layer carrying
# at least one part whose conversion factor has width, or has no figure to say whether it does.
#
# A derived total settles like a filed one
#   A composed layer that files its demand or nameplate as its fusion's sum is read at the figure
#   its fusion computes (layers/demand.sqlc, layers/nameplate.sqlc), and where its conversions are
#   point values that figure differences as legitimately as a filed one: the eliminations are
#   inside it, subtracted bound by bound, so `n - d` over it is the pivot over its parts. So it is
#   not here, and a remainder walk stops on it. Where its figure cannot be computed the walk stops
#   on a node with no figure, which composition/suspended_remainders.sqlc lifts and
#   algebra/settled_remainders.sqlc counts.
#
# It reads the resolved parts, not the references, and that keeps two grounds apart
#   `composition/part_references.sqlc` would carry a part whose notation names no filing here, and
#   such a fusion is already lifted by `composition/unresolved_parts.sqlc`. Reading references here
#   would suspend it twice, on two grounds, one of which is not about conversions at all. Composing
#   `composition/parts.sqlc` is what keeps this ground and that one disjoint.
#
# A factor with no figure is not a point
#   A conversion filed `unmeasured`, or as an identity's output nothing here computes, has no
#   width anybody can read, and comparing its two empty bounds would read it as a point: a blank
#   taken for the value that makes `n - d` legitimate. Nobody knows whether the factor scaled both
#   totals with a spread, so both states are unsettled by name, and only a stated factor is asked
#   for its width.
#
# The base case of the remainder recursion
#   This is the base case of the remainder recursion, stated properly.
#   `composition/leaves.sqlc` is where the part graph bottoms out, and it is the base case for a
#   sum over parts. A remainder bottoms out earlier: it stops wherever `n - d` is legitimate, and
#   that is wherever the two were not scaled by one factor with width. A leaf qualifies trivially,
#   having nothing above it that could have correlated anything, but so does every composed layer
#   whose conversions are point values, and the corpus is almost entirely those.
#
# A remainder descent stops at the first settled node
#   So a descent for a remainder must stop at the first settled node and not run to the leaves.
#   Running past one throws away a filed figure its composer stands behind, and with it every
#   correction that composer already applied, which then has to be rebuilt from below and can fail
#   for reasons the filed figure had already settled.
-- composition/parts.sqlc restricted to the parts whose factor has width or no figure.
SELECT DISTINCT p.composition AS filing, p.composed_layer AS layer
FROM (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) p
WHERE p.factor_state IN ('absent', 'derivation')
   OR (p.factor_state = 'stated' AND p.factor_low <> p.factor_high)
